  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The performance and superiority of the schemes are demonstrated through mathematical proofs and numerical experiments on simulated transport networks rather than real-world empirical deployments.",
      "what": "This study formulates and compares link- and path-based incentive schemes designed to reduce urban traffic congestion under budget constraints using non-linear optimization with complementarity constraints. The researchers mathematically proved that the feasible region of the link-based model is a subset of the path-based model, and developed a column generation-based iterative algorithm to solve both models. Numerical experiments across various transport networks were conducted to evaluate the algorithm's scalability and the models' performance in reducing total travel time.",
      "why": "It mathematically establishes that path-based incentives are inherently more effective than link-based incentives at shifting user equilibrium toward a system optimum under the same budget, providing a theoretical foundation for designing more efficient credit or subsidy programs in urban traffic management."
    }
  },
